1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,503
A deploy pipeline goes red on a Friday
evening. The engineer on call says, "The fix

2
00:00:09,543 --> 00:00:14,608
is committed. Look, here's the commit." The
pipeline runs again, and fails again, on

3
00:00:14,648 --> 00:00:17,950
the same line. Then your CTO
asks three calm questions.

4
00:00:17,990 --> 00:00:21,231
What exactly is in the commit
that the pipeline built?

5
00:00:21,271 --> 00:00:26,153
Where does that commit exist right now:
on the laptop, on the server, or on both?

6
00:00:26,193 --> 00:00:31,135
And what's the smallest change that makes
the main branch correct, and how will we

7
00:00:31,175 --> 00:00:32,047
know it worked?

8
00:00:34,387 --> 00:00:37,997
Notice something? None of those
is a question about commands.

9
00:00:38,037 --> 00:00:42,904
Each one is a question about state: which
bytes Git recorded, which names point at

10
00:00:42,944 --> 00:00:47,811
them, and which machine holds them. An
engineer who knows fifty commands and can't

11
00:00:47,851 --> 00:00:52,060
answer those questions will try things
until something appears to work.

12
00:00:52,100 --> 00:00:56,848
An engineer who can answer them needs
about ten read-only commands and a method.

13
00:00:56,888 --> 00:00:59,241
This course teaches the second engineer.

14
00:01:01,081 --> 00:01:04,370
Welcome to Git and GitHub Deep
Mastery. Pull up a chair.

15
00:01:04,410 --> 00:01:09,244
This first video has no Git theory. It sets
up three things that every later video

16
00:01:09,284 --> 00:01:13,880
depends on: the way the book and the
videos are written, the lab sandbox where

17
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:18,694
you'll type every command, and the fixed
clock that makes the output on my screen

18
00:01:18,734 --> 00:01:20,656
equal to the output in your book.

19
00:01:20,996 --> 00:01:26,070
The goal of the course fits in one
sentence. You'll learn to diagnose a Git or

20
00:01:26,110 --> 00:01:31,316
GitHub problem from first principles,
choose the lowest-risk fix, verify that it

21
00:01:31,356 --> 00:01:34,791
worked, and prevent it from
happening silently again.

22
00:01:34,831 --> 00:01:39,840
Four verbs: diagnose, choose, verify,
prevent. Notice what's not on the list?

23
00:01:39,880 --> 00:01:40,954
Knowing commands.

24
00:01:41,294 --> 00:01:46,011
By the end of these sixteen minutes you'll
have a working sandbox, and you'll know

25
00:01:46,051 --> 00:01:50,188
that nothing in this course reads or
writes your real Git configuration.

26
00:01:50,228 --> 00:01:54,945
You'll also be able to say why the commit
ID on my screen is the commit ID in your

27
00:01:54,985 --> 00:01:58,078
textbook. Hold that thought
until the end of the demo.

28
00:01:59,918 --> 00:02:01,690
Here's that promise as a checklist.

29
00:02:02,030 --> 00:02:03,442
After this video you can:

30
00:02:03,782 --> 00:02:08,874
State the goal of the course in one sentence:
diagnose from first principles, choose

31
00:02:08,914 --> 00:02:11,134
the lowest-risk fix, verify, prevent.

32
00:02:11,474 --> 00:02:18,442
Open an isolated lab shell with labs/shell,
replay a demo with labs/run, and check a

33
00:02:18,482 --> 00:02:21,111
chapter with labs/verify-all.sh.

34
00:02:21,451 --> 00:02:26,388
Explain why the lab configuration is isolated,
and name the variable that redirects

35
00:02:26,428 --> 00:02:28,187
the global configuration file.

36
00:02:28,527 --> 00:02:34,669
Explain why replays pin the clock, and why
the sandbox sets the two gc.reflogExpire

37
00:02:34,709 --> 00:02:36,010
settings to never.

38
00:02:36,350 --> 00:02:40,402
Give the meaning of the three risk labels
and the five questions asked before a

39
00:02:40,442 --> 00:02:41,334
dangerous command.

40
00:02:43,174 --> 00:02:47,533
So that's the destination. Now, why
is the course built the way it is?

41
00:02:47,873 --> 00:02:52,838
The ladder. Every important concept in the
textbook is taught on the same ladder,

42
00:02:52,878 --> 00:02:57,163
and these videos climb it too. In
one sentence: the plain explanation.

43
00:02:57,203 --> 00:03:00,561
Analogy: one comparison, and
the place where it breaks.

44
00:03:00,601 --> 00:03:04,207
Precisely: the technical
statement, with the correct terms.

45
00:03:04,247 --> 00:03:07,543
Inside the dot git folder:
which files change on disk.

46
00:03:07,883 --> 00:03:13,191
See it: a real transcript. Picture: a
diagram. In production: a realistic case from

47
00:03:13,231 --> 00:03:18,603
a backend team or an AI and ML team. The
ladder goes from plain words, down to files

48
00:03:18,643 --> 00:03:23,822
on disk, and back up to a real team. When
you can walk a concept down and up that

49
00:03:23,862 --> 00:03:25,626
ladder yourself, you own it.

50
00:03:25,966 --> 00:03:31,129
Transcripts are real. Whenever you see
terminal output in a text block in the book,

51
00:03:31,169 --> 00:03:36,208
or on my screen, it's real output. A script
in the labs folder produced it, and a

52
00:03:36,248 --> 00:03:39,719
tool inserted it into the
page. Nobody typed it by hand.

53
00:03:39,759 --> 00:03:44,798
In those transcripts, $LAB stands for the
lab root, which is the git-mastery-labs

54
00:03:44,838 --> 00:03:49,563
folder in your home directory, unless
you set the variable GIT_MASTERY_LABS.

55
00:03:49,903 --> 00:03:54,582
A line in square brackets that says exit
status and a number is the exit status of

56
00:03:54,622 --> 00:03:59,302
the command above it. And a line that starts
with a hash sign is a comment written

57
00:03:59,342 --> 00:04:00,108
by the script.

58
00:04:00,448 --> 00:04:05,471
Three tools. You run all three from the
course folder, the directory that contains

59
00:04:05,511 --> 00:04:08,064
the textbook, labs and lab-manual folders.

60
00:04:08,404 --> 00:04:14,008
The first is labs/shell m00. It opens your
shell in the hands-on directory for m00,

61
00:04:14,048 --> 00:04:19,040
under the lab root, with an isolated
Git configuration and the real clock.

62
00:04:19,080 --> 00:04:23,324
This is where you type labs by
hand, and typing exit leaves it.

63
00:04:23,364 --> 00:04:28,697
The second is labs/run ch01/first-repo.
It replays one demo or lab with a fixed

64
00:04:28,737 --> 00:04:34,341
identity and a fixed clock, prints the
transcript, and leaves the sandbox under the

65
00:04:34,381 --> 00:04:37,537
lab root so that you can
inspect it afterwards.

66
00:04:37,877 --> 00:04:43,490
The third is labs/verify-all.sh ch01. It
replays every script of a chapter and

67
00:04:43,530 --> 00:04:47,693
compares the output with the
snippets printed in the book.

68
00:04:47,733 --> 00:04:51,244
For each script it prints
PASS, VOLATILE or FAIL.

69
00:04:51,584 --> 00:04:56,504
Why the configuration is isolated. Git
reads its settings from several files, and

70
00:04:56,544 --> 00:05:00,852
one of them is your personal dot
gitconfig file in your home directory.

71
00:05:00,892 --> 00:05:05,628
Think about both directions. If the labs
read that file, your aliases and your

72
00:05:05,668 --> 00:05:09,853
defaults would change the output,
and my screen wouldn't match yours.

73
00:05:10,193 --> 00:05:15,112
If the labs wrote that file, an experiment
in a lab would change your real setup.

74
00:05:15,152 --> 00:05:20,070
So the lab environment points Git at a
configuration file inside the sandbox, and

75
00:05:20,110 --> 00:05:23,682
switches off the system-wide
file. Three variables do this.

76
00:05:23,722 --> 00:05:28,089
GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL names the file Git
reads instead of your personal one.

77
00:05:28,429 --> 00:05:33,973
GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM, set to one, makes Git
skip the system-wide configuration file.

78
00:05:34,013 --> 00:05:39,557
GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES makes Git stop
searching for a dot git directory at the lab

79
00:05:39,597 --> 00:05:43,257
root, so a sandbox never acts
on a repository above it.

80
00:05:43,597 --> 00:05:48,255
Why the clock is fixed. A commit records
when it was made, and the commit ID is

81
00:05:48,295 --> 00:05:51,822
computed from everything in the
commit, including that time.

82
00:05:51,862 --> 00:05:56,223
So the same commands, run at two
different moments, produce different IDs.

83
00:05:56,563 --> 00:06:01,517
That's why replays pin the identity and
the clock. The lab clock starts on Monday

84
00:06:01,557 --> 00:06:06,696
the seventh of September 2026, at ten in
the morning, five and a half hours ahead of

85
00:06:06,736 --> 00:06:09,902
UTC, and it advances one
minute before each command.

86
00:06:10,242 --> 00:06:14,859
That's why the IDs printed in the book
are the IDs you get from a replay.

87
00:06:14,899 --> 00:06:20,217
One catch: in labs/shell the clock is real,
so commits you type by hand get IDs that

88
00:06:20,257 --> 00:06:24,874
differ from the book. That's expected,
and Chapter 6 explains it in full.

89
00:06:25,214 --> 00:06:30,209
Risk labels. The first time a state-changing
command appears in a chapter, or in a

90
00:06:30,249 --> 00:06:32,542
video, it carries one of three labels.

91
00:06:32,882 --> 00:06:36,519
🟢 SAFE: the command reads
state or only adds objects.

92
00:06:36,559 --> 00:06:42,278
Nothing is lost. The book's examples are
git status, git log, git fetch, git reflog

93
00:06:42,318 --> 00:06:43,319
and git commit.

94
00:06:43,659 --> 00:06:47,961
🟡 CAUTION: the command moves
refs or rewrites local history.

95
00:06:48,001 --> 00:06:51,797
It's recoverable through the
reflog, if you know how.

96
00:06:51,837 --> 00:06:56,067
Examples: git reset --soft, git
rebase, git commit --amend.

97
00:06:56,407 --> 00:07:02,256
🔴 DANGEROUS: the command can destroy
uncommitted work, remote history, or the safety

98
00:07:02,296 --> 00:07:08,145
net itself. Examples: git reset --hard, git
clean -fd, and git push --force together

99
00:07:08,185 --> 00:07:13,263
with its conditional forms. Yes, we
all know a teammate who force-pushes.

100
00:07:13,303 --> 00:07:15,367
We'll meet them later, kindly.

101
00:07:15,707 --> 00:07:20,529
The labels describe the worst case. git
commit is green because it only adds, and a

102
00:07:20,569 --> 00:07:25,156
wrong commit is corrected by another
commit. git reset --hard is red because it

103
00:07:25,196 --> 00:07:30,077
overwrites working-tree files that were
never recorded anywhere, and Git can't bring

104
00:07:30,117 --> 00:07:31,600
back what it never stored.

105
00:07:31,940 --> 00:07:36,771
Before any red command, the book answers
five questions, and so will I, out loud,

106
00:07:36,811 --> 00:07:41,582
before I run it: what it changes, what it
can destroy, how to preview it, how to

107
00:07:41,622 --> 00:07:45,190
recover, and when it's appropriate.
Make that your own rule.

108
00:07:45,230 --> 00:07:50,002
If you can't answer the five questions,
you're not ready to run the command on a

109
00:07:50,042 --> 00:07:51,445
repository that matters.

110
00:07:51,785 --> 00:07:56,465
GitHub-side material. What happens on
GitHub's servers can't be replayed on your

111
00:07:56,505 --> 00:08:01,008
Mac. Those parts of the course show
commands without output, and describe the

112
00:08:01,048 --> 00:08:04,371
expected result from GitHub's
documentation, with a link.

113
00:08:04,411 --> 00:08:08,796
For the GitHub chapters you create a
free organization with public practice

114
00:08:08,836 --> 00:08:13,575
repositories yourself. Nothing in this
course asks you to type credentials into a

115
00:08:13,615 --> 00:08:13,988
script.

116
00:08:15,828 --> 00:08:20,207
One picture holds all of that. The
lab is a sealed room on your own Mac.

117
00:08:20,547 --> 00:08:25,737
Inside the room there's a Git configuration
file that belongs to the room, a clock

118
00:08:25,777 --> 00:08:29,564
that I control during replays,
and one directory per replay.

119
00:08:29,604 --> 00:08:34,730
Outside the room is everything you care
about: your real dot gitconfig file, your

120
00:08:34,770 --> 00:08:39,768
real repositories, your credentials.
labs/shell and labs/run both walk into the

121
00:08:39,808 --> 00:08:41,235
room, and nowhere else.

122
00:08:41,575 --> 00:08:46,802
So where does the model break? In two
places. First, the room has two doors with

123
00:08:46,842 --> 00:08:52,200
different clocks. labs/run uses the fixed
clock, and labs/shell uses the real one.

124
00:08:52,240 --> 00:08:56,611
So "inside the sandbox" doesn't
always mean "same IDs as the book".

125
00:08:56,651 --> 00:09:02,141
Second, the fixed clock isn't airtight. Two
parts of Git ignore it and read the real

126
00:09:02,181 --> 00:09:06,815
clock, and the sandbox needs two
configuration lines to deal with that.

127
00:09:06,855 --> 00:09:12,279
The diagram shows the reason in a root-cause
box, the first of many in this course.

128
00:09:14,119 --> 00:09:15,206
Let's draw that room.

129
00:09:15,546 --> 00:09:20,405
The outer box is your Mac. On the
left is your real configuration file.

130
00:09:20,445 --> 00:09:24,200
No arrow reaches it: it's
never read and never written.

131
00:09:24,240 --> 00:09:29,789
On the right is the lab root, which holds
the sandbox configuration, the hands-on

132
00:09:29,829 --> 00:09:35,516
directories, and one directory per replay.
Two arrows enter it, one from labs/shell

133
00:09:35,556 --> 00:09:39,242
and one from labs/run. Nothing
points at the left box.

134
00:09:39,582 --> 00:09:44,072
Read it with me, and notice the form:
seven lines, always the same seven.

135
00:09:44,112 --> 00:09:49,097
The mechanism is the line to remember: two
parts of Git compare reflog dates with

136
00:09:49,137 --> 00:09:54,247
the real clock, not with the commit dates.
A lab date in the future trips the first

137
00:09:54,287 --> 00:09:58,652
rule, and a lab date in the past trips
the second once it's old enough.

138
00:09:58,992 --> 00:10:05,201
So the lab clock is kept in the past, and
time-based reflog expiry is switched off

139
00:10:05,241 --> 00:10:10,002
in the sandbox. That's why you'll
see gc.reflogexpire=never and

140
00:10:10,042 --> 00:10:16,023
gc.reflogexpireunreachable=never in every
sandbox configuration listing in this

141
00:10:16,063 --> 00:10:16,556
course.

142
00:10:16,896 --> 00:10:22,554
Three things follow. In a replay, your
commits always behave like fresh work, on

143
00:10:22,594 --> 00:10:28,181
whatever day you run it. Real repositories
use Git's defaults, which Chapter 13

144
00:10:28,221 --> 00:10:34,021
covers, and the labs demonstrate only
explicit expiry, which doesn't depend on any

145
00:10:34,061 --> 00:10:38,864
clock. And the variable GIT_TEST_DATE_NOW
pins date arithmetic only.

146
00:10:38,904 --> 00:10:44,205
git fsck and reflog expiry read the
real clock regardless, which is why the

147
00:10:44,245 --> 00:10:50,046
configuration lines are needed as well.
The git fsck rule exists in Git 2.53.0 and

148
00:10:50,086 --> 00:10:54,248
later. Apple's Git 2.50.1 on
the same Mac doesn't apply it.

149
00:10:56,088 --> 00:11:01,165
Enough about the room. Let's walk in. From
the course folder, replay the smoke test.

150
00:11:01,505 --> 00:11:05,847
Before the output appears, predict
which file the settings will come from.

151
00:11:05,887 --> 00:11:08,985
Your home directory, or
somewhere under the lab root?

152
00:11:09,025 --> 00:11:09,933
Say it out loud.

153
00:11:10,273 --> 00:11:15,398
Look at the second column. Every global
line comes from a dot gitconfig file under

154
00:11:15,438 --> 00:11:20,689
the lab root. The local lines come from the
repository's own config file, inside its

155
00:11:20,729 --> 00:11:23,713
dot git folder. Your own
file isn't in the list.

156
00:11:24,053 --> 00:11:26,341
Now the script that shows the room itself.

157
00:11:26,681 --> 00:11:31,356
The first snippet lists the Git environment
that the lab library sets for every

158
00:11:31,396 --> 00:11:31,773
replay.

159
00:11:32,113 --> 00:11:35,699
Read the variables against
the table of section 1.7.

160
00:11:35,739 --> 00:11:41,346
GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL points into the sandbox,
so Git reads this file instead of your

161
00:11:41,386 --> 00:11:46,505
personal one. GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM=1
means the system-wide file is skipped.

162
00:11:46,545 --> 00:11:52,291
And GIT_CEILING_DIRECTORIES makes Git stop
searching for a dot git directory at the

163
00:11:52,331 --> 00:11:55,707
lab root. Those three are
also set in labs/shell.

164
00:11:56,047 --> 00:12:01,332
GIT_AUTHOR_DATE and GIT_COMMITTER_DATE
are the fixed clock, in Git's raw format:

165
00:12:01,372 --> 00:12:06,058
seconds since the first of January
1970 UTC, then the time-zone offset.

166
00:12:06,098 --> 00:12:11,649
GIT_TEST_DATE_NOW is a variable that Git's
own test suite uses to fix "now" for date

167
00:12:11,689 --> 00:12:17,107
arithmetic, so that relative dates such as
"2 minutes ago" give the same answer on

168
00:12:17,147 --> 00:12:22,100
every run. The name and email variables
are a fixed identity that overrides

169
00:12:22,140 --> 00:12:23,031
configuration.

170
00:12:23,371 --> 00:12:29,282
GIT_EDITOR=true, GIT_PAGER=cat and
GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0 mean no editor, no pager

171
00:12:29,322 --> 00:12:33,616
and no password prompt, because
a script can't answer them.

172
00:12:33,656 --> 00:12:39,125
GIT_MERGE_AUTOEDIT=no means a merge
doesn't open an editor for its message.

173
00:12:39,165 --> 00:12:44,929
In labs/shell, the clock, the identity
variables and the editor settings aren't

174
00:12:44,969 --> 00:12:50,952
pinned: the clock is real, the identity
comes from the lab configuration, and your

175
00:12:50,992 --> 00:12:52,569
editor and pager work.

176
00:12:52,909 --> 00:12:57,569
Next, the script lists the configuration,
sets a global alias, and lists the

177
00:12:57,609 --> 00:13:02,455
configuration again. git config set
--global carries the label 🟡 CAUTION in the

178
00:13:02,495 --> 00:13:07,403
book's command-safety table, because it
writes your personal configuration file.

179
00:13:07,443 --> 00:13:10,990
A slightly nervous prediction:
where will that alias land?

180
00:13:15,830 --> 00:13:21,601
--show-scope and --show-origin print the
scope of every setting and the file it came

181
00:13:21,641 --> 00:13:25,821
from. Look at the last line:
alias.st is in the sandbox file.

182
00:13:25,861 --> 00:13:30,594
Inside the sandbox, a "global" setting
is global only to the sandbox.

183
00:13:30,634 --> 00:13:34,260
Your real configuration was
neither read nor written.

184
00:13:34,300 --> 00:13:40,071
You can breathe out. And there are the two
gc.reflogexpire lines from the root-cause

185
00:13:40,111 --> 00:13:40,348
box.

186
00:13:40,688 --> 00:13:43,977
Last, the clock, and the promise
from the start of this video.

187
00:13:44,017 --> 00:13:47,413
The script makes one empty commit
and prints it with both dates.

188
00:13:47,753 --> 00:13:52,823
Look at AuthorDate and CommitDate: Monday
the seventh of September 2026, a few

189
00:13:52,863 --> 00:13:57,212
minutes after ten, at plus five
thirty. Now the commit ID, c672627.

190
00:13:57,252 --> 00:14:01,339
Open section 1.7 of the textbook:
the same ID is printed there.

191
00:14:01,379 --> 00:14:06,383
It'll be the same on your Mac when you
replay the script, today or next year.

192
00:14:06,423 --> 00:14:11,820
Don't take my word for it. Compare it
yourself, because that comparison is the test

193
00:14:11,860 --> 00:14:13,130
that your lab works.

194
00:14:14,970 --> 00:14:18,170
Five mistakes to watch for,
each with its root cause.

195
00:14:18,510 --> 00:14:23,931
Expecting the book's commit IDs in labs/shell.
Root cause: labs/shell uses the real

196
00:14:23,971 --> 00:14:29,261
clock, and the commit ID is computed from
everything in the commit, including the

197
00:14:29,301 --> 00:14:29,590
time.

198
00:14:29,930 --> 00:14:36,510
Running the tools from the wrong directory.
Root cause: labs/shell, labs/run and

199
00:14:36,550 --> 00:14:42,964
labs/verify-all.sh are run from the course
folder, the directory that contains

200
00:14:43,004 --> 00:14:45,694
textbook/, labs/ and lab-manual/.

201
00:14:46,034 --> 00:14:50,429
Believing that GIT_TEST_DATE_NOW
freezes all of Git's time.

202
00:14:50,469 --> 00:14:56,592
Root cause: it pins date arithmetic only;
git fsck and reflog expiry read the real

203
00:14:56,632 --> 00:15:01,027
clock, which is why the two
gc.reflogExpire settings exist.

204
00:15:01,367 --> 00:15:05,930
Reading a risk label as the typical outcome.
Root cause: the labels describe the

205
00:15:05,970 --> 00:15:10,763
worst case; green means nothing can be lost,
not that the result is what you wanted.

206
00:15:11,103 --> 00:15:15,641
Assuming a lab can show what GitHub does.
Root cause: what happens on GitHub's

207
00:15:15,681 --> 00:15:19,983
servers cannot be replayed on your Mac;
those parts are described from the

208
00:15:20,023 --> 00:15:20,805
documentation.

209
00:15:22,645 --> 00:15:27,649
Now, out of the lab. Two engineers on an
evaluation team type the same Git command

210
00:15:27,689 --> 00:15:32,755
on two Macs, and see different behavior.
One has an alias and a personal default in

211
00:15:32,795 --> 00:15:37,922
their dot gitconfig file. The other doesn't.
Neither knows, because neither has ever

212
00:15:37,962 --> 00:15:42,966
listed where their settings come from. The
command you saw in the demo, git config

213
00:15:43,006 --> 00:15:47,949
list --show-origin --show-scope, is where
that difference shows: every setting in

214
00:15:47,989 --> 00:15:50,102
effect, and the file it comes from.

215
00:15:50,442 --> 00:15:55,119
The lab removes this whole class of
confusion by giving every replay one known

216
00:15:55,159 --> 00:15:59,714
configuration file. On your team you
can't isolate everyone's configuration.

217
00:15:59,754 --> 00:16:03,705
So you do the next best thing: you
look at it before you theorize.

218
00:16:05,545 --> 00:16:11,556
Your turn. Do Lab 0.1, "Verify the
toolchain and build the sandbox", in

219
00:16:11,596 --> 00:16:15,390
lab-manual/m00-lab-setup.md.
Type it by hand.

220
00:16:15,730 --> 00:16:20,713
Before you start, write down two
predictions. First: when the lab lists the

221
00:16:20,753 --> 00:16:25,534
configuration inside the sandbox,
which file will the global lines name?

222
00:16:25,574 --> 00:16:30,958
Second: when you make a commit by hand in
labs/shell m00, will its ID equal an ID

223
00:16:30,998 --> 00:16:34,507
printed in the book? Check
both against what you see.

224
00:16:34,547 --> 00:16:37,186
And if one was wrong, good. Explain why.

225
00:16:39,026 --> 00:16:44,099
Q15: "The same commands typed at two
different times produce different commit IDs.

226
00:16:44,139 --> 00:16:49,087
Why? What did the lab environment pin to
prevent it, and what did it not need to

227
00:16:49,127 --> 00:16:49,399
pin?"

228
00:16:49,739 --> 00:16:52,852
Answer it out loud before
you open the answers file.

229
00:16:52,892 --> 00:16:57,945
A strong answer says what a commit ID is
computed from, and names time as one of the

230
00:16:57,985 --> 00:17:01,705
inputs. It names what the replays
pin, and by which mechanism.

231
00:17:01,745 --> 00:17:06,798
And it reasons about the last part of the
question instead of guessing: which inputs

232
00:17:06,838 --> 00:17:10,921
to an ID are already identical on
two machines, without any pinning?

233
00:17:10,961 --> 00:17:14,135
If that part feels out of
reach today, that's normal.

234
00:17:14,175 --> 00:17:17,045
You'll be able to answer
it fully after video 7.

235
00:17:18,885 --> 00:17:23,112
Let's land this. You should now
be able to say these sentences.

236
00:17:23,152 --> 00:17:28,801
The goal of this course is to diagnose from
first principles, choose the lowest-risk

237
00:17:28,841 --> 00:17:34,422
fix, verify, and prevent. Every transcript
is real output from a script in the labs

238
00:17:34,462 --> 00:17:38,892
folder, and I can replay it with
labs/run and check a chapter with

239
00:17:38,932 --> 00:17:40,179
labs/verify-all.sh.

240
00:17:40,519 --> 00:17:46,047
The lab never touches my real configuration,
because GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL redirects the

241
00:17:46,087 --> 00:17:50,355
global file and GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM
switches off the system file.

242
00:17:50,395 --> 00:17:55,857
Replays pin the clock because a commit ID
depends on time, and the sandbox sets two

243
00:17:55,897 --> 00:18:01,226
reflog-expiry settings to never, because
git fsck and expiry read the real clock.

244
00:18:01,266 --> 00:18:06,330
A risk label describes the worst case,
and before a red command I answer five

245
00:18:06,370 --> 00:18:06,993
questions.

